From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: musa <musa@local>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 10:40:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] MUSA: route QSA prefill to TileLang

Route QSA prefill through the TileLang sparse paged GQA kernels that the
previous commit brought over, and apply the output gate the Triton epilogue
uses after whichever attention path ran.

The kernels tile the grouped-query axis, so they are only selected when the
step fits one tile: 16 rows for prefill, 8 for split-k decode. The guard also
requires BF16 and a single KV head per rank. Prefill is unconditional, as on
the v0.28 branch; decode stays behind VLLM_MUSA_QSA_TILELANG_DECODE because
its split-k scratch and merge kernel have not been measured on v0.32 yet.

Prefill sends the requests whose sequence fits one selection to
FlashAttention and keeps the sparse kernel for the rest, which is what the
earlier branch settled on: a dense request costs one varlen call instead of a
selection walk over its whole prefix. The split needs the padded v0.32
metadata, so the dense layout also zeroes the key count of the padded
requests whose sequence length is stale.

Build the kernels and allocate the split-k scratch in the layer constructor,
before graph capture. The decode path is first reached while the decode
graphs are being captured, so a lazy build there would be recorded into the
graph memory pool and alias another captured size.

The block-table width and the page stride are read off the tensors, so the
packed v0.32 layout that stores K and V for one token side by side works
without a cache-layout change.
